---
source: misc/tests/help.rs
expression: "help_output(\"getino\")"
---
Print the inode number of a process's pidfd or one of its namespaces, given a PID.

The kernel never reuses a pidfs inode during the same boot, so the PID:INODE form (-p) refers to
that process without races, for example with kill PID:INODE. Namespace inodes match the ones shown
in /proc/PID/ns/TYPE.

Usage: getino [OPTIONS] <PIDS>...

Arguments:
  <PIDS>...
          Process IDs. An optional :INODE suffix is accepted and ignored

Options:
      --pidfs
          Print the pidfs file-descriptor inode (default)

      --cgroupns
          Print the cgroup namespace inode

      --ipcns
          Print the IPC namespace inode

      --netns
          Print the network namespace inode

      --mntns
          Print the mount namespace inode

      --pidns
          Print the PID namespace inode

      --timens
          Print the time namespace inode

      --userns
          Print the user namespace inode

      --utsns
          Print the UTS namespace inode

  -p, --print-pid
          Prefix output with the PID, separated by a colon

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
